::: {.callout-industry}
**Industry Application:** In automotive transmission manufacturing, bearings
require precise interference fits to prevent spinning on the shaft while
allowing the bearing to rotate freely. Companies like Ford and GM specify fits
to the micrometer level, and incorrect fits can lead to premature bearing
failure and warranty claims.
:::

### Key Takeaways {-}

- Fits define the relationship between mating parts (hole and shaft)
- Hole basis systems are more common because holes are easier to standardize
- Clearance fits allow movement; interference fits create permanent joints
- Transition fits may result in either slight clearance or interference
- The metric ISO system uses letters (uppercase for holes, lowercase for shafts)
  combined with IT grades to specify tolerances
- Always select from preferred fits when possible to reduce manufacturing costs

::: {.callout-check}
**Quick Check:** For a bearing that needs to rotate freely on a shaft, would
you specify a clearance fit or an interference fit? What class of fit (RC, LC,
FN, etc.) might be appropriate?
:::
